course: dictionary
lesson: 7434
title: Pollinators: the helpers that carry pollen
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A pollinator is an animal, usually an insect like a bee, butterfly or moth, that carries pollen from one flower to another. Pollen is the dusty powder that flowers make. When pollen moves between flowers, the plant can make seeds and fruit.
explain: Pollinators are not only insects. «Some birds (like hummingbirds), bats and even the wind can act as pollinators.» So a pollinator is anything that moves pollen from flower to flower, not just the animals you might expect.
explain: Use the clues in a sentence to work out a new word. «Apples, almonds, strawberries and coffee all need pollinators.» These foods only grow after pollen has moved between flowers, so pollinators must help plants make fruit and seeds.
explain: Some words in science are used in everyday life too. «Attract pollinators» means to bring pollinators in, for example by planting flowers they like. When you read a new word, ask yourself what job it does in the sentence.
fact: A pollinator carries pollen from one flower to another.
fact: «Pollinators» include bees, butterflies, moths, hummingbirds, bats and the wind.
fact: Honeybees are kept by beekeepers, but many wild bees and other pollinators are declining because of lost flowers, pesticides, disease and climate change.
fact: Many wild bees live alone in little holes, not in hives, so they need wildflowers close by.
exercise: «Apples, almonds, strawberries and coffee all need pollinators.» What is a pollinator? | *A flower that has no pollen | A kind of fruit tree | A tool used to pick fruit | A thing that carries pollen from flower to flower
exercise: «Bees carry pollen from flower to flower so that fruit can form.» What does pollen do here? | It stops fruit from growing | It turns into honey straight away | It makes flowers change colour | *It moves between flowers so fruit can form
exercise: Mia says the wind is not a pollinator because it is not alive. Is she right? | Yes, because wind is a kind of insect | No, because the wind is a bird | Yes, only living things are pollinators | *No, the wind can carry pollen, so it can act as a pollinator
test: «Some birds (like hummingbirds), bats and even the wind can act as pollinators.» Which of these is NOT named as a pollinator? | Bats | The wind | *Earthworms | Hummingbirds
test: Schools plant «pollinator gardens» to help bees. Why? | To make the garden darker | To stop flowers from making seeds | *To attract pollinators and give them flowers | To keep bees away from the children
exam: «Many bees have become fewer because of lost wild flowers, some pesticides, disease and changing weather.» What does this tell us about pollinators? | *Some pollinators are in danger and need our help | Bees never need flowers | Pollinators are only found in shops | Pollinators are getting bigger every year
